text:
alex_support: Hey team — customer reported streaming responses from the Node/TS SDK arriving in a big burst instead of incremental chunks on Vercel Edge runtime. They see normal tokens for ~5s then a huge chunk — looks like buffering.

maria_eng-runtime: Which SDK + runtime versions? streaming behavior can differ between SDK releases and Vercel's edge image.

alex_support: Customer said: redwood/sdk-js@0.9.2, node 18 (edge), Vercel platform (edge functions). They were using the top-level `client.chat.stream()` helper. Logs show repeated 200s but client only gets data after EOF.

nate_devex: 0.9.x had a different streaming transport; we added a streaming polyfill + edge-specific path in 0.10.x/0.11.x that uses native ReadableStream on edge. Ask them to try upgrading first.

alex_support: Repro steps they gave:
```
import Redwood from '@redwood/sdk';
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
const res = await client.chat.stream({ model: 'redwood/fast-13b', input: 'tell me a story' });
for await (const chunk of res) console.log('chunk', chunk);
```
They run this inside an edge handler on Vercel and see `chunk` printed only once after ~4-6s.

maria_eng-runtime: Sounds like Vercel's edge worker is buffering the response from origin until a certain threshold unless the runtime uses platform streaming APIs. Two tracks: 1) SDK upgrade to use edge-native streaming, 2) confirm the deployment target is 'edge' (not serverless) and that Vercel's streaming is enabled in project config.

alex_support: I asked for network captures — customer pasted this header set from their edge worker -> our API: `connection: keep-alive`, `accept: text/event-stream`, `authorization: Bearer <redacted>`. No chunked transfer-encoding on the upstream request (we expect chunked for SSE).

nate_devex: Minor but important: on Node edge runtimes you MUST set `accept: text/event-stream` and let the SDK open the request as streaming. Also ensure the build target doesn't polyfill fetch in a way that removes streaming. Some frameworks swap fetch implementations (e.g., older Next/Vercel adapters).

lisa_cust-success: Customer is blocked — they rely on streaming latency for UX. Any hotfix we can suggest before they upgrade?

maria_eng-runtime: Quick mitigation: ask them to force the SDK to use the fetch-based streaming path (if available) or use a minimal fetch wrapper as a repro. Example shim for edge: 
```
// edge-stream-repro.js
export default async function handler(req) {
  const upstream = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Accept': 'text/event-stream', 'Authorization': `Bearer ${process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY}` },
    body: JSON.stringify({ model: 'redwood/fast-13b', input: 'story' })
  });
  const reader = upstream.body.getReader();
  const decoder = new TextDecoder();
  while (true) {
    const { value, done } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;
    const chunk = decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
    // Immediately forward to client/console
    console.log('got chunk', chunk);
  }
  return new Response('ok');
}
```
This isolates SDK and shows whether Vercel is buffering origin streams.

alex_support: Customer tried a similar minimal fetch and saw incremental chunks. So SDK path is the problem for them (older SDK variant). They can patch to call raw fetch as above short-term.

nate_devex: For a targeted fix: we should check for version mismatch where the bundler rewrites `ReadableStream` polyfills or our streaming transport falls back to XHR-like buffered mode. Maria, can you look at runtime logs for that customer request id=cid_7f8a? devops_bot might have trace.

devops_bot: trace cid_7f8a: origin response streamed with Transfer-Encoding: chunked; payload started at t=0.14s relative to request, chunks at 0.2s intervals. SDK gateway buffered until upstream closed (t=5.9s) before emitting.

maria_eng-runtime: Confirmed. The gateway proxy layer for that SDK version lacked streaming flush for edge JS bundles. We'll patch a gateway shim to forward chunks immediately. I'll open an internal PR and attach to a hotfix branch.

alex_support: In the meantime, suggested steps for customer:
- Upgrade to @redwood/sdk-js >= 0.11.0 (streaming fixes)
- If upgrade not possible, use the minimal fetch shim above as a workaround
- Ensure deployment target is edge and no fetch polyfills are injected (check bundler config)
- Verify REDWOOD_API_KEY is provided as env var and not replaced at build-time (should be runtime-only)

lisa_cust-success: I'll relay the upgrade + shim option. Customer asked about retry semantics when a connection drops mid-stream — do we have guidance?

maria_eng-runtime: Yes: streaming responses are typically non-idempotent unless you design them to be. Recommend: small checkpoints / tokens in your prompts or use short lived deltas + resume tokens is future work. For now, implement client-side retry with exponential backoff and detect partial streams. Example: restart stream and dedupe by server-generated request_id header. We will add a request_id header in the hotfix.

nate_devex: Also file a docs note about Vercel edge gotchas + list of supported SDK versions for streaming. I'll draft it and link to the PR.

alex_support: Thanks — marking this thread actionable. I'll follow up with the customer once PR lands or they upgrade. :thumbsup:
